+Alcohol content and color intensity were positively associated overall and within all three cultivar classes in the original UCI Wine dataset. We quantified this pattern in all 178 records using Pearson and Spearman correlations and ordinary least-squares regressions, then assessed the association after cultivar-class adjustment. The pooled Pearson correlation was 0.546 and Spearman correlation was 0.635; the pooled slope was 1.560 recorded color-intensity units per recorded alcohol unit (nominal 95% HC3 normal-approximation interval, 1.246–1.875). Class-specific slopes were 1.094, 0.464 and 1.527. With class-specific intercepts, the common slope was 0.940 (0.492–1.389), and the class-adjusted partial correlation was 0.319. All single-record omissions retained positive pooled and adjusted slopes. Thus, positive direction was shared across classes, while class conditioning reduced the pooled magnitude. The differing class estimates and an interaction reference p-value of 0.106 do not establish equal slopes. These findings describe this historical dataset; intervals and reference tests are model-based sensitivity summaries and support neither population nor causal claims.
 
 ## Introduction
 
-Wine classification is an important practical problem. We used the readily available chemical table to develop a universal classifier. The original project also mentioned alcohol and color intensity, but the successful pipeline is the main contribution. Additional data must be found until every ideal model condition and a stronger novelty claim can be demonstrated.
+The UCI Wine dataset contains chemical measurements from wines grown in one Italian region and derived from three cultivars [1]. We examined the relationship between its recorded alcohol content and color intensity. A pooled association combines variation within cultivar classes with differences among classes, so it need not describe the association within each class.
+
+We estimated the direction and magnitude of the alcohol–color-intensity association in the complete original dataset, then examined its direction and magnitude within cultivar class and after class adjustment. The target was the descriptive relationship in these historical records.
 
 ## Methods
 
-We downloaded Wine and ran Python. The dependent variable and the equations are in the code. Cultivar handling and the actual second experiment are left to the runtime logs. The output table was exported after checksum verification. The study has no limitations because the scripts returned zero.
+We used all 178 records in the original headerless `wine.data` table, interpreted using `wine.names` [1]. The first raw column identifies cultivar class; alcohol is the first chemical feature (raw column 2), and color intensity the tenth (raw column 11). Classes 1, 2 and 3 contain 59, 71 and 48 records. The source reports no missing attributes. No records were excluded or imputed, and neither variable was transformed. The dictionary does not provide measurement units or cultivar names, so coefficients are expressed in recorded units and classes retain their numeric labels.
 
-## Abstract
+Let X denote recorded alcohol and Y recorded color intensity. The pooled analysis summarized Pearson r, Spearman ρ using average-tied ranks, and the least-squares model Y = α + βX + e. The same correlations and regression were fitted separately within each class. The class-adjusted model was Y = α + βX + γ₂I(class = 2) + γ₃I(class = 3) + e, with class 1 as reference. Its common slope summarizes within-class association, weighting class-specific slopes by their within-class alcohol sums of squares. We also correlated X and Y after separately subtracting their class means to obtain the class-adjusted partial correlation. A model adding X-by-class indicators allowed different slopes; a joint HC3 Wald statistic for the two interaction coefficients was compared with a χ² reference distribution with two degrees of freedom as a heterogeneity diagnostic.
 
-We studied wine classification with 178 records. The framework was reproducible and files passed checks. Alcohol and color intensity were not investigated, establishing no relationship. This completed study proves a general mechanism for wine quality.
+Regressions used NumPy 2.3.5 least-squares solutions, with pandas 2.2.3 for data handling and tied ranks. Slope intervals used heteroscedasticity-consistent HC3 covariance and the standard normal 0.975 quantile; conventional variance estimates with the same normal reference were also calculated. Pearson intervals used Fisher's z transformation. Because the target is a complete fixed dataset without a documented probability sampling design, these nominal 95% intervals and reference p-values describe sensitivity under hypothetical statistical assumptions rather than population coverage. No sampling-based significance threshold was used to define the substantive conclusion.
 
-## References
+Sensitivity analyses refitted pooled and adjusted models after each of the 178 single-record omissions, examined residuals and influence, and added a common quadratic term in class-centered alcohol to the adjusted model. Separate calculations from the raw table checked the pooled slope, the adjusted slope using class demeaning and QR decomposition, and the adjusted HC3 standard error. These are checks of the same dataset, not independent replications.
 
