+## Results
+
+The pooled association was positive: Pearson r = 0.546 (Fisher nominal 95% interval, 0.434–0.642), Spearman ρ = 0.635, and slope = 1.560 (HC3 interval, 1.246–1.875). The pooled regression R² was 0.299.
+
+Positive associations were also present within every class, although their magnitudes differed (Table 1). Class-specific slopes ranged from 0.464 in class 2 to 1.527 in class 3. Alcohol means were 13.745, 12.279 and 13.154 in classes 1–3, while color-intensity means were 5.528, 3.087 and 7.396, respectively.
+
+**Table 1. Alcohol–color-intensity associations in the original Wine records.** Slopes are recorded color-intensity units per recorded alcohol unit; brackets contain nominal 95% HC3 normal-approximation intervals. Each row uses all records in its stated analysis set.
+
+| Analysis set | n | Pearson r | Spearman ρ | Slope [HC3 interval] |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| All records | 178 | 0.546 | 0.635 | 1.560 [1.246, 1.875] |
+| Class 1 | 59 | 0.408 | 0.443 | 1.094 [0.423, 1.765] |
+| Class 2 | 71 | 0.270 | 0.257 | 0.464 [0.078, 0.850] |
+| Class 3 | 48 | 0.350 | 0.284 | 1.527 [0.295, 2.759] |
+
+After class adjustment, the common slope remained positive at 0.940 (HC3 interval, 0.492–1.389), and the class-adjusted partial correlation was 0.319. The adjusted model R² was 0.622 for alcohol and class indicators together. Conventional normal-reference slope intervals were 1.207–1.914 pooled and 0.525–1.356 adjusted. The interaction diagnostic was χ²(2) = 4.483, with reference p = 0.106; it does not establish that the class slopes are equal.
+
+Across all single-record omissions, pooled slopes ranged from 1.492 to 1.604 and adjusted slopes from 0.818 to 0.998; all remained positive. Maximum Cook's distances were 0.099 and 0.140, respectively. Correlations between absolute residuals and fitted values were 0.188 and 0.481, indicating residual variation that warrants attention to the variance assumptions. The added class-centered quadratic coefficient was 0.014 (HC3 interval, −0.652–0.680), leaving substantial uncertainty about curvature. Separate raw-file calculations reproduced the main slopes to a maximum absolute difference of 1.33 × 10⁻¹⁵ and the adjusted HC3 standard error to 3.36 × 10⁻¹⁴.
+
+## Discussion
+
+Alcohol content and color intensity were positively associated in this dataset, both overall and within each of its three cultivar classes. The association persisted after class adjustment, with a smaller common slope and partial correlation than their pooled counterparts. Cultivar conditioning reveals a smaller within-class summary while preserving the positive direction. Reporting both pooled and class-conditioned estimates therefore gives a fuller description of the relationship.
+
+Consistency of direction should be distinguished from equality of magnitude. The three fitted slopes differ numerically, and the adjusted common slope is a weighted summary rather than a demonstration of identical class responses. The interaction diagnostic supplies neither an equivalence test nor a population-level conclusion. Rank correlations and the omission analyses support the positive descriptive direction, while the wide quadratic interval does not establish linearity.
+
+The original records lack a documented probability sampling frame, collection dates, batch structure, replication details and measurement units. HC3 addresses heterogeneous error variance under independent errors; it cannot remedy unknown clustering, measurement error or an inappropriate mean model. Accordingly, these coefficients summarize the recorded wines and do not identify a causal mechanism, establish practical wine-quality implications or justify extrapolation to other wines. 
+
+Within this historical dataset, alcohol and color intensity share a positive association overall and conditional on cultivar class. Its smaller magnitude after adjustment is the central distinction between the pooled and class-conditioned descriptions.
+
